mely worcester  warriors are a rugby club which has been  at the heart of our county and community  for decades and they follow in the  footsteps of the worcester rugby  football club which has played rugby  union in worcester for over 150 years  in the era of professional rugby which  roughly coincides with my adult lifetime  the club has been based at six ways and  throughout my adult life i've been a  supporter the first game of professional  rugby i ever watched was in worcester  then in north midlands division two and  although never a player myself i've worn  the club colours of golden blue ever  since when i gave worcester rugby shirts  to my two nephews then aged four and  eight they described them as their uncle  robin's suits as they had to often see  me wearing mine as for so many other  local folk the club has provided a forum  for intergenerational bonding an arena  for local pride in the gathering space  for pressure at special events the rise  and rise of worcester who subsequently  became the warriors was no accident but  the result of the vision and drive of  one man worcester's most successful 20th  century entrepreneur and philanthropist  for late great cecil duckworth it isn't  possible to overstate cecil's  contribution to our city the boiler he  first made in his garage became the  prototype of the modern combi boiler and  the basis for worcester heat systems now  known as worcester bosch the biggest  private sector employer in my  constituency his endowment of the  duckworth worcestershire trust continues  to make an enormous contribution to our  local environment and his generous  support for acorns children's hospice  made their worcester hospice a reality  his greatest and most prominent local  legacy however was the rise of the  warriors i was privileged long before i  became worcester's mp to know cecil and  his family and to be able to watch rugby  at six ways with him i recall watching a  pre-season friendly between worcester  and oxford university while i was a  student there and learning that even  great figures such as cecil and his  opposite number at the university rugby  team who happened to be a former head of  the civil service and distinguished  member of the other place were capable  of colourful language when the referee's  decision went against their team  i celebrated with him an astonishing six  successive league wins and promotions as  with his support the warriors as they  became in 2002 moved all the way up from  north midland division two to national  league one the league just beneath the  rugby premiership and i will remember  the ecstatic feeling when our team  unbeaten after 26 wins in 26 matches  first one promotion to the top flight in  the 2003 full season like so many fans i  experienced the pain of relegation in  2009 followed by joy at return to the  top flight in my first year as  worcester's mp  all of this was masterminded by cecil  and his passion to see the club not just  achieve but cement their position at the  top of english rugby when i first  attended six ways there was one stand  with capacity of around two thousand  today we have a twelve thousand capacity  stadium which is not only one of the  best equipped professional rugby  stadiums in the country but also a venue  for low key local cultural events from  concerts to the trooping of the mercian  regiment's colour quite rightly a bust  of cecil adorns the warrior stadium and  he was named life president of the club  before his sad death from cancer in 2020  while some might say this is just a  sports club in worcester we know it's  much more than that so many fans have  spoken out about what the club means to  them and the staff and heads of  department as well as the players have  shown a spirit of togetherness in the  toughest of times of which settle  himself would be proud i don't have time  to echo all the sentiments of fans in  this short debate but so many have  expressed what the club means to them  movingly and with real passion i would  commend to the house looking at the  hashtags together we are warriors and  save our warriors on social media  the club is also home to one of the most  effective and successful community  foundations in the rugby world a key  part of settle's vision and the  foundation reaches more than fifteen  thousand deprived and vulnerable people  across the west midlands championing  accessible rugby delivering innovative  and inspiring lessons in schools  including special schools and  alternative provision using the power of  rugby to build confidence and unlock  opportunity i've lost count of the  number of times i've been downstairs in  this place to congratulate the  foundation on winning awards at the  premier rugby community awards sadly all  of this is now at risk  the current owners of the club have  brought it to the brink of financial  collapse and for all that they claim  that this is the impact of the pandemic  they have failed to maintain the trust  of their employees keep their promises  to local stakeholders or set out clear  plans to reassure their many creditors  their background in property development  and the various complex transactions  through which they have maneuvered parts  of the club and its land have raised  serious doubts about their genuine  commitment to keeping professional rugby  at six ways the news was on 17th of  august they were served with a winding  up notice by hmrc led dozens of my  constituents to contact me with their  concerns about the future of the club on  the 26th of august i convened a meeting  of local mps with council leaders from  all three southwestern councils and the  county to discuss the concerns about the  possibility of development land being  separated from the club and risks of a  viability of the stadium and the team  we agreed a joint statement including  crucially the leaders of which haven  council the planning authority and  worcestershire county council with its  responsibilities for economic  development and that read we will do all  we can to  retain professional elite rugby at six  ways and protect the extraordinary  legacy of the late cecil duckworth and  his family we jointly call on the  worcester warriors owners to act in the  best interests of the club the players  the staff the fans and the community  served by the club including the  warriors community foundation we think  it is essential that the club and all of  its property assets remain linked whilst  we recognize that there are significant  opportunities for development of the  six-way site we believe that these need  to be utilized for the purposes of  sustaining the rugby club and the wider  ambitions of the local sporting  community we are all very clear we are  prepared to work supportively with  potential investors to find a positive  outcome for the future of worcester  warriors  since that statement was published i'm  grateful to have messages of support  from worcester's labour mayor city  councillors the supporters trust and the  president of the amateur side wrfc i'm  also grateful for the close attention  that has been paid to the situation by  the rfu premier rugby limited and dcms  over recent weeks and particularly for  the patients of the current secretary of  state with the bombardment of messages  i've been sending her ever since her  appointment her predecessor set out to  me that the sole focus of the department  has been in trying to protect the club  and future of professional rugby at six  ways amen to that  following our statement local mps were  invited to meet with the current owners  and hear their plans we accepted  assurances that they were negotiating to  sell the whole of the club together and  but whatever formal structures were in  place around the land there was no  intent to separate or sell parts of it  to the document detriment of the club we  were told that the club was in  negotiations with a number of parties  and that payroll would certainly be made  the following week  the following week the owners failed to  make payroll staff were not paid at all  on the day their wages were due and  players did not receive their pay on  time this triggered players at the club  to serve 14 days notice that their  contracts had been breached posing an  existential threat to the continuation  of the team and professional rugby at  six ways on the same day mobile phones  and management at the club stopped  working as the bills hadn't been paid  and cars were taken away from players  because leases had not been maintained  academy players were reportedly made  homeless as they lost access to their  accommodation  in the days of confusion and deep  concern that followed the players were  eventually paid late and sometimes  irregularly but together selflessly they  decided to withdraw their notice and  return to being in contract the staff of  whom around 200 of permanent full-time  staff and a further 200 part-time were  offered 65 of their wages with the rest  to follow once the deal had been secured  this has so far not been forthcoming and  i'm told there are still a number of  staff who've received no pay at all  it was at this stage that the five  worcester mps who were free to do so put  out our joint statement calling for the  club to be taken into administration i  know all six of us were there in spirit  the owners fired back an angry release  but stressed all the risks of  administration and stated that they had  to have no offers of help from mps or  councils prior to our statement the  latter i have to say is simply a  provable lie  their case against administration was  four-fold but it would reduce the value  of the club's p share its share of  proceeds from premiership television and  marketing rights due to a call option  being available to prl to buy it back in  the event of administration but it would  leave local creditors out of pocket but  it would lead to automatic relegation  from the premiership and that it would  lead season tax ticket holders without  the value of their tickets each of these  assertions is challengeable and from my  own conversations with prl and the rfu i  know that neither triggering the call  option on the p-share nor relegation  should be considered a certainty  i urge them to do all they can in the  event of an orderly administration to  enable warriors to stay in the  premiership with a point deduction if  necessary and to ensure that any new  management and investors taking the club  on have access to its pshare there is no  reason why an administrator or new  investor should not be able to honor  season tickets and local suppliers who  from bitter experience have no trust in  the current owners to pay their bills  may stand a greater chance of recouping  some of what they're owed through an  orderly process  through continued uncertainty and  disorder  since that time i'm afraid the season  the situation off the pitch has not  improved players have gone above and  beyond to turn out and play for the club  despite the problems with their pay  staff have moved heaven and earth to  ensure that games can go ahead meeting  the challenges set by the rfu and prl  even after wi-fi and internal emails  went down with no support from their  directors and owners but worcester  warriors players have scored tries  against london irish exeter chiefs and  gloucester is a remarkable achievement  in these most difficult possible  circumstances and the solidarity that's  been shown by each of those clubs  reflects the desire of all rugby fans to  see the warriors survive but the  university of worcester warriors the  lady team ladies team actually won their  alliance cups fixture against harlequins  is truly spectacular the heroic efforts  of underpaid or unpaid staff have been  praised by fans of clubs across the  country but they have barely been  acknowledged by the current owners  instead we've had reports of staff  facing disciplinary action for daring to  point out the string of broken promises  that have been made to them and of key  people being mysteriously unavailable  when legal or insurance documentation  needed to be signed  through all of this the team under the  tutelage of steve diamond has maintained  a spirit of unity that is admirable in  the extreme  the owners told local mps last week that  they were on the brink of a deal to sell  85 percent of the club's equity and that  there would be new money to repay staff  the proportion of wages owing and secure  all the commitments to a premiership  before the end of last week they  promised staff and fans an announcement  within 48 hours of the match on sunday  neither of these promises have been kept  staff fans and players at the club are  left with the lingering doubt that the  owners might prefer the club to default  on its rugby commitments so that  expulsion from the premiership makes it  easier to focus on developing property  assets away from the rugby such an  outcome would risk making not only the  warriors but the community foundation  the academy the amateur club wrfc and  the worcester raiders football club  homeless it will be a disaster for sport  in our county and a huge blow which  either i nor my fellow worcestershire mp  are prepared to accept  even after staff went above and beyond  the gain to secure this weekend's  matches  another deadline has understandably been  set by the rugby authorities for monday  and i know that staff players and the  exhausted heads of department of the  club will do all they can to meet it but  i cannot be certain that they will be  able to without the support of the  directors or new finance if the process  gives way i will give back i'm very  grateful my honorable friend from  worcester making fantastic case the  importance of rugby in his city as in  mine gloucester and can i just share  with him the solidarity that everybody  at king's home in gloucester rugby feels  for the club we want to see the warriors  back on great form and we want to see  these financial problems resolved he has  our full support in gloucester i'm  extremely grateful to my honourable  friend for gloucester and i have to say  as the son of a former gloucester player  it made me very proud and  impressed when when gloucester offered  free tickets uh to that game um  just the other day um to the staff and  those players of worcester warriors who  weren't playing i think that was a great  gesture of solidarity and it was  enormously  appreciated if if the protestations of  the current owners are true that they  have the best interests of the club at  heart then surely even at this stage  they should be calling in the  administrators however whilst any doubt  persists about their motivation i now  urge dcms as the largest creditor and  the department responsible for  safeguarding the interests of sport to  step in and do so before monday i know  of at least two significant interested  parties one of whom is the party with  whom owners claim to be about to strike  a deal last week who have said they are  interested in stepping in with new  finance to support the club but only  through a process of administration  i say to my honourable friend the  minister that this now seems the only  way forward before he responds i do just  want to address two further points that  have been brought to my attention by the  press firstly there is a suggestion from  one creditor of the club that sport  england have somehow unwittingly  assisted in the separation of assets  from the club or made it easier for  property to be alienated from it i hope  my honourable friend can assure me this  is not the case  in doing so i would urge him not simply  to reiterate that there's already been a  formal separation of the stadium from  other land before the sport england loan  was negotiated we all know that but it's  not the point the concern is that the  new lease that was negotiated at sport  england's behest changed the terms on  which the rugby trading company held use  of the stadium and reduced its access to  non-rugby income and the proceeds of any  events other than those related to the  game itself the accounts show that prior  to this the book value of the lease held  by the trading company was 16 million  pounds can wearable thing confirm that  this book value still sits with the club  and the assets over which the dcms has a  call and if not i hope you can reassure  me that any process of administration  will take into account of all uses of  public funding and where any of this has  been used to pay property debt or secure  other assets for the owners or their  holding companies mq property limited  six ways property limited and bond group  property limited six ways these can be  brought into scope of any administration  process  i do not believe for a moment that sport  england or anyone at the department wish  to reduce the income available to a  sports club but i do think it's vital we  ensure that no inadvertent harm is done  through the complex processes the club  has gone through under departmental  supervision finally  and most damningly in the eyes of most  worcester folk is the report in today's  mail that the owners borrowed money from  the family of the late cecil duckworth  and have failed to repay it i cannot  stress enough how upsetting and  appalling this is one senior player has  described the suggestion as  heartbreaking  what is also striking having now  discussed the matter with beatrice  cecil's widow is that the money was  borrowed in january 2020 before any  impact of the pandemic and long before  the owners admitted to the currently  financial woes of the club with the  express intention of making payroll  within a few years of taking control of  the club and after one of their original  investors pulled out they went to the  great founder and benefactor of  warrior's success and borrowed half a  million pounds  since his death they refused to  communicate with his widow or her  lawyers to give an update as to the  status of this debt or confirm when and  how it might be repaid they have  asserted that half the money is not owed  as a promise was made on the basis of a  handshake for cell to cover the costs of  employing the then manager of the club  allen sonomas and although there is no  documentary evidence to back this claim  the family have accepted that they will  not contest it  even after this there has been no  further engagement with the duckworth  family on the remaining money i cannot  express in parliamentary terms my  personal revulsion at the way in which  those charged with protecting cecil  duckworth's legacy have behaved and  seemingly continue to behave  as i'm told the loan doesn't appear  anywhere in the published accounts of  the club all the holding companies it  also begs questions as to how they are  meeting their legal responsibilities as  directors and what other uncle  undeclared debts they may have taken on  no wonder one potential buyer has this  week called for administration to  include quote a forensic investigation  of financial activities  my request to the minister is therefore  simple two weeks ago i and my fellow  worcestershire colleagues spoke out with  one voice to call on the dcms to step in  and take the warriors into  administration in order that their  future can be secured that call is now  more urgent than ever nothing in the  experience of the last two weeks has  given us any greater confidence that the  current directors can or will deliver  the patience of staff players and fans  is being stretched beyond endurance  investors are waiting in the wings with  serious offers backed by serious local  business people and serious rugby folk  to take the club out of administration  and set it on a secure footing securing  their support is vital i urge the rfu  and prl to continue to show the  forbearance and understanding that  they've shown to date and to listen to  the calls from across the rugby world  for the way be found to allow the  warriors to continue to play in the top  flight i urge dcms to delay no longer  further and to formally trigger a  process of administration to secure the  club and all the property assets  associated with it before monday's  deadline i urge them to ensure there are  directors in charge of the warriors who  are fit and proper in short minister  please save our warriors  thank you very much indeed madam deputy  speaker and i'm pleased to respond to  this debate and i'm grateful to my  honorable friend for worcester for  securing uh for securing this debate the  interest shown i think this afternoon is  testament to the important  importance that this club represents to  the local community and to the sport of  rugby as a whole and i pay tribute for  the work that he has done and can i also  offer  my  thanks at also to my honourable friend  the member for mid worcestershire who um  did this job extremely well and i know  that i've got very big shoes to fill so  i  and i know that he has taken is now able  to take keen interest in this  issue as we've heard today  the club has had many different forms  but can date back to worcester rugby  football club which was first  established in 1871 it was a long and  eventful journey to the club's debut in  the men's top flight in 2004 under the  stewardship of john brain and cecil  duckworth who he's talked so movingly  about today both of whom have had a  lasting impact on the club and the local  community the club has gone from  strength to strength and seen its talent  recognized at an international level  with multiple players including current  captain ted hill being capped for  england the dramatic extra time the  premiership cup win against london irish  in may provided an unforgettable moment  for all involved with the club  the success is not just limited though  to the men's team however with the  worcester  ladies team winning their inaugural  premiership title in 2013 before  becoming part of warriors group in 2016.  the success has continued since then  with laura keats and lydia thompson both  being named for the england women's  world cup squad this week  and off the pitch the warriors community  foundation makes a significant impact  around the local area providing vital  services including a positive and safe  learning environment for some of the  hardest to reach young people  for all these reasons i was pleased that  the government was able to support the  club to survive the challenges of the  kobit 19 pandemic through the sport  survival package like many other sectors  the sports sector suffered as a result  of the essential restrictions we all  lived under during the pandemic the  government was proactive in taking  action to protect the sector through the  600 million pound sport survival package  the package was set up to ensure as many  sport clubs  reliant on spectators survive the period  of restrictions during the pandemic  whilst also seeking to minimise the  potential long-term damage to sport with  a particular focus on the importance of  grassroots activity and women's sport  this intervention was essential in  maintaining professional sport in this  country through such a very difficult  period  however as the nation recovers and the  crowds return to stadiums it is right  that the government takes a step back  from providing direct financial support  the support survival package was  administered by sport
 

